[Theorem thm: convergence of the polymer measure of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	Under the same assumptions as Theorem (thm: convergence of the polymer partition function of the paper), for every $\hat{\beta} \in [0,\infty)$, there exists a random probability measure ${\ensuremath{\mathbf Q}} ^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}$ on $\mathcal{D}$ such that the following convergence in distribution holds in ${\ensuremath{\mathcal M}} _1(\mathcal{D})$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathrm{P}_{N}^{\omega,\beta_N} \xrightarrow[\ N \to +\infty\ ]{(d)} {\ensuremath{\mathbf Q}} ^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}\,.
	\end{equation*}
	This convergence holds jointly with the convergence of the discrete noise to $\boldsymbol\zeta$. 
	We refer to the measure~${\ensuremath{\mathbf Q}} ^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}$ as the \emph{continuum long-range directed polymer model with \(\gamma\)-stable Lévy noise}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

