body:
[Definition 23 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Integral molecules $INT(Mol)$]
Given $m \in Obj(Mol)$. $m$ is called an \textit{integral molecule} if 
\begin{equation*} 
A \rightarrow_M m. 
\end{equation*} 
$INT(Mol)$ denotes the collection of all integral molecules, i.e.,
\begin{equation*} 
INT(Mol):=\{m \in Obj(Mol) \ |\  A \rightarrow_M m\}.
\end{equation*} 
The collection INT(Loop) of all \textit{integral loops} and the collection INT(Reg) of all \textit{integral regions} are defined by
\begin{align*}
INT(Loop):&=\{ l \in Obj(Loop)\  |\  A \rightarrow_L l\},\\
INT(Reg):&=\{ |m| \in Obj(Reg)\ |\  m \in INT(Mol) \}.
\end{align*}

[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2606.01832, 'MA(1) processes with uniform innovations conditioned to stay positive in the non-expanding'] In the blue region, as $n\to\infty$, it holds for $y\in[-a,1]$ that
    \begin{align*}
       p_n^{a,y}(\theta)\sim \frac{a+1}{E\left(\theta,\frac{-\theta }{\lambda(a+1)}\right)}\, \lambda^{n+1}E\left(\theta,\frac{-\theta y}{\lambda(a+1)}\right),
    \end{align*}
    where $\lambda=\lambda(\theta,a)$ is given as the smallest real positive root equation of \eqref{root_blue}.

