body:
[Proposition PROP: gaussian-conc of arXiv:2411.07840, 'Central limit theorem for the focusing $Φ^4$-measure in the infinite volume limit'] There exists a constant $c>0$ such that for sufficiently small $\delta >0$
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}_{\nu_{Q, x_0}^\perp }\Big[ e^{\delta \int_{-L^2}^{L^2} |h^\perp|^2 \,\mathrm{d}x } \Big] \lesssim e^{c L^2},

\end{align}

\noindent
uniformly in $x_0 \in \mathbb{T}_{L^2}$. In particular, we have the probability estimate
\begin{align*}
\nu^{\perp}_{Q, x_0}(D_L^c) \lesssim  e^{- c D L^2},
\end{align*}

\noindent
uniformly in $x_0 \in \mathbb{T}_{L^2}$, where $D_L=\big\{ \| h^\perp \|_{L^2(\mathbb{T}_{L^2})}^2 \le DL^2 \big\}$ and $D$ is chosen to be sufficiently large.

