[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2609.00736, 'Modalities in non-classical variations of S4'] For all formula $\varphi$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \neg\neg\neg\varphi\leftrightarrow \neg \varphi$;
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \Box\Box\varphi \leftrightarrow \Box\varphi$;
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \Diamond\Diamond \varphi \leftrightarrow\Diamond\varphi$;
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \Box\Diamond\Box\Diamond \varphi \leftrightarrow \Box\Diamond\varphi$;
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \Diamond\Box\Diamond\Box \varphi \leftrightarrow \Diamond\Box \varphi$;
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \Box\neg \neg\Box\neg  \varphi \leftrightarrow \Box\neg \varphi$; and
        \item  
            $\csf\vdash \Box\neg\Box\neg\Box\neg\Box\neg \varphi \leftrightarrow \Box\neg\Box\neg\varphi$.
    \end{enumerate}

[Proposition p:tightness of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] Consider a family of random edge-rooted graphs $(G^{\lambda}, E^{\lambda})_{\lambda \in \Lambda}$ in $\mathcal{G}$, and let $\deg(E^\lambda)$ denote the sum of degrees of endpoints of $E^{\lambda}$. This family is tight if and only if
  \begin{equation}

    \deg(E^{\lambda}), \text{ for $\lambda \in \Lambda$},
  \end{equation}
  is tight. The same result also holds for any family of random edge-rooted graphs with marks in $\mathcal{A}$, $(G^{\lambda}, E^{\lambda}, \mathsf{m}^\lambda)_{\lambda \in \Lambda}$ in $\mathcal{G}_\mathcal{A}$.

