{"artifact_digest":"sha256:7ce156e22ea50765aabdb9398bc62ee77ed0629eaa90818067030b4f753f1075","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/7c/7ce156e22ea50765aabdb9398bc62ee77ed0629eaa90818067030b4f753f1075.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:7ca936592888f10549432e1501567c0c42694e840e23c56aa22b43363a7555c2","bundle_entry_key":"2606-19115-lemma-8-normalized-derivatives-coeffic","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_054","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_054.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-19115-lemma-8-normalized-derivatives-coeffic","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We prove the formula for \(D_n^d\); the proof for \(\partial_n^d\) is analogous.
The case \(d=0\) is immediate. Assume the claim holds for \(d-1\), and set
\(m:=n-d+1\). Then
\[
D_n^{d-1}p(z)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{m}
(-1)^k \binom{m}{k}\coef{k+d-1}{n}(p)\,z^{m-k}.
\]
Applying \(D_m\), we get
\begin{align*}
D_n^d p(z)
&=D_m(D_n^{d-1}p)(z)=
\sum_{k=0}^{m}
(-1)^k \binom{m}{k}
\left(\frac{m-k}{m}-1\right)
\coef{k+d-1}{n}(p)\,z^{m-k}\\
&=
-\sum_{k=1}^{m}
(-1)^k \frac{k}{m}\binom{m}{k}\coef{k+d-1}{n}(p)\,z^{m-k}.
\end{align*}
