[Proposition 10 (H\"older estimates for the transformed coefficients) of arXiv:2608.29726, '\LARGE A Priori Estimates for Singular Fractional Stochastic Burgers Equations'] Assume the hypotheses of \Cref{prop:response,prop:zvonkin} and let
\(0<\rho\le\beta<1\).  Then the coefficients in
\eqref{eq:coefficients} satisfy
\begin{align}
  [a]_{C^\beta}
  &\lesssim_\Psi\norm{J-1}_{C^\beta},
  \\
  \norm{Q_0}_{C^\rho}
  &\lesssim_\Psi
  \norm{\ell}_{C^\rho}
  \bigl(1+\norm{J-1}_{C^\beta}\bigr),
  \\
  \norm R_{C^\rho}
  &\lesssim_\Psi\norm{\ell}_{L^\infty}\norm{\ell}_{C^\rho},
  \\
  \norm{Q_\lambda}_{C^\rho}
  &\lesssim_\Psi \norm{Q_0}_{C^\rho}
  +\lambda\norm{\psi}_{C^{1+\beta}}.

\end{align}
The implicit constants are locally uniform on bounded sets of maps with a
fixed positive Jacobian lower bound.

[Thm exactform of arXiv:2605.10452, 'A secretary for Messrs. Luce and Mallows'] {\bf(P-1)}
Consider the secretary problem for a Mallows distribution in the case that the largest number is of highest rank.
For  $n\in\mathbb{N}$  and $q\in(0,\infty)-\{1\}$,
\begin{equation}
P_n^{\mathcal{M};q}(\mathcal{S}^\uparrow(n,M))=\begin{cases}\frac{1-q}{1-q^n}q^{n-M-1}(1-q^M)\sum_{j=M+1}^n\frac1{1-q^{j-1}},\  M\in\{1,\cdots, n-1\};\\
\frac{1-q}{1-q^n}q^{n-1},\ M=0.\end{cases}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

