[Theorem thm local LILZ of arXiv:2411.15681, 'Strassen's local law of the iterated logarithm for the generalized fractional Brownian mot'] {Assume $\gamma\in (0,1)$ and $\alpha\in(-1/2, 1/2)$}. Then   for every  $t_0>0$,
   with probability one, the family $\left\{U_{t_0, h}
   (x)\right\}_{x\in [0,1]} $ defined by (equation eq UZ of the paper)  is 
   relatively compact on $C([0,1])$ as  $h\rightarrow 0 $, and
   its set of limit points is $\mathcal S$ defined by 
   (equation eq unit ball of the paper). Namely, for every $t_0>0$,  
    \begin{align}
 \lim_{h\rightarrow 0 }\inf_{f\in \mathcal S} \|U_{t_0, h}-f \|_{\infty}=0 \ \ \ \ \text{a.s.},
 \end{align}
 and for each $f\in \mathcal S$,
    \begin{align}
 \liminf_{h\rightarrow 0 } \|U_{t_0, h}-f \|_{\infty}=0 \ \ \ \ \text{a.s.}
 \end{align}
  In particular,  for every continuous functional $F: C([0,1])\rightarrow \mathbb R$,
 \begin{equation}
 \mathbb P\bigg(\limsup_{h\rightarrow0} F(U_{t_0, h})=\sup_{\xi\in \mathcal S}F(\xi) \bigg)=1.
 \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

