[Theorem thm:packet of arXiv:2608.29403, 'Kac's Walk on Rotation Matrices Mixes in $\boldsymbol{\Theta(n^2)}$ Steps:\\ A Proof Disco'] Fix a global circuit state $\tau$ before any component is estimated.  Let
$P$ be a connected pure-Weyl component with $m_P\ge2$ coefficient
occurrences and $c=c^{\!*}(P)\ge2$, and retain all of its declared cut
interfaces.  Its raw physical open tensor has a finite presentation
$\zeta_{P,\tau}\in\mathscr P_\tau$ satisfying
\[
 \boxed{
  \|\zeta_{P,\tau}\|_{\mathscr P_\tau}
  \le C_1^{m_P}n^{c-2}
       \|C_P\|_2^2\|C_P\|_\mathrm{op}^{m_P-2}.}
\tag{2.5.1}
\]
Every predeclared interface occurs once.  This is a projective
presentation cost.  It is not an ordinary norm of a separately evaluated
open $K_5$ component; evaluation and circuit normalization are deferred
to (lem:circuit-output of the paper).

[Theorem 69 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Under Assumptions~\ref{Assumptions_Theta} and \ref{Assumptions_basic}, there exists a constant $C$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions, such that for any $t > 0$ and $\N$ large enough we have
\begin{equation}

 \P\big[\Dr[\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_\N,\boldsymbol{\mu}]\geq t\big] \leq \exp(C\N\log\N - \N^2t^2).
\end{equation}

