Integrate this inequality against $\mu$ to get $-\phi'(z)\ge z^{-2}$.  For the second bound, differentiate $\widetilde\phi=c\phi+(1-c)/\zeta$ to get $-\widetilde\phi'(z)=c(-\phi'(z))+(1-c)z^{-2}\ge z^{-2}$, using $c\le1$.  Since $\phi(z),\widetilde\phi(z)\ge z^{-1}$, the product rule gives the last","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:4cbdac31a0b85040c24eedb11adcddecf9b8e806e1f0e09a51d5cad1025664f2","revision_digest":"sha256:9ce4cfbe74fa98b1db8b1959283913a38f3e889d18449e24b944822453f2dac7","source_id":"arxiv:2608.19594","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

