[Definition def:zero of arXiv:2605.03896, 'Dimer models on astroidal zig-zag graphs'] We say that a point~$(x,y)\in \mathcal D_c^\circ$ lies in a \emph{phase region} if it belongs to a rough, smooth, frozen, or quasi-frozen region. For such a point~$(x,y)$, let
\begin{equation} 
\zeta_*=\zeta_*(x,y) := \begin{cases} \Omega(x,y), &\text{if $(x,y) \in \mathcal L$},\\
\text{any point of $A_{(x,y)}$}, &\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

[Theorem 56 (Low-endpoint Wick coefficient equivalence) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $m\ge1$, $1\le r\le2$, and $1\le p<\infty$.  There are constants
$0<\widetilde a_{m,p,r}\le\widetilde b_{m,p,r}<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}

 \widetilde a_{m,p,r}
 \norm K_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\Sigma}}
 \le\norm{I_m(K)}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \le\widetilde b_{m,p,r}
 \norm K_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\Sigma}}
\end{equation}
for every $K\in\mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\Sigma}$.  Hence the Wick evaluator is
an isomorphism onto a closed subspace.

[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2603.25452, 'New approach to optimal control of delayed stochastic Volterra integral equations'] Let $\hat{u}$ be the optimal solution to the consumption problem \eqref{OP}. Then, we derive
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{u}(t)&=& \left(\mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbb{E}^{\mathbb{Q}}\left(\Psi(t)\theta\middle|\mathcal{F}_t\right)|\mathcal{G}_t\right]\right)^{-1}\\
&=& \left(\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\mathbb{E}\left(\Psi(t)M(T)\theta\middle|\mathcal{F}_t\right)}{M(t)}\middle|\mathcal{G}_t\right]\right)^{-1},
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\mathbb{Q}$ is a probability measure defined by
\begin{eqnarray}
	 d\mathbb{Q}=M(T)d\P
\end{eqnarray}
 with
\begin{eqnarray*}
	M(t)=\exp\left(\int_0^t\sigma_0(s)dB(s)-\frac{1}{2}\int_0^t\sigma_0^2(s)ds\right).
\end{eqnarray*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:J^V momentum map of arXiv:2608.28278, 'Reduction of symmetric time-dependent Hamiltonian systems I: presymplectic principal $\R$-'] The momentum map $J:T^*Q \to \mathfrak{g}^*$ induces an $Ad^*$-equivariant momentum map $J^V: V^*\Pi \to \mathfrak{g}^*$ characterized by
    \begin{equation}
        J = J^V \circ \mu,
    \end{equation}
    which makes $(V^*\Pi, \Omega_h, V^*\phi, J^V)$ a symmetric presymplectic Hamiltonian system.

