[Proposition prop:sobolev_curvature of arXiv:2608.24963, 'Why and When Neural Networks Improve Local Approximation in Optimization'] (Adapted from Proposition~3.1 in [Taminiau2025Arxiv2502].)
Let $m_\theta:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}$ be twice continuously differentiable. Fix $z\in\mathbb{R}^n$ and $h>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
m_\theta(z)=f(z),\qquad m_\theta(z+h e_\ell)=f(z+h e_\ell),\ \ell=1,\dots,n.
\end{equation}
Define $g_h(z)$ by forward differences (equation eq:fd_forward of the paper). Then there exist $\zeta_1,\dots,\zeta_n\in[0,1]$ such that
\begin{equation}
\left\lVert g_h(z)-\nabla m_\theta(z) \right\rVert_2^2=\frac{h^2}{4}\sum_{\ell=1}^n\left([\nabla^2 m_\theta(z+h\zeta_\ell e_\ell)]_{\ell\ell}\right)^2.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 19 (Averaged off-diagonal gain on a fixed contour) of arXiv:2608.07820, 'Linear Spectral Statistics for Entrywise-Transformed Spiked Wigner Matrices under Shifted '] Let \(A=A^{\mathsf T}\) and assume
\(\operatorname{dist}(\Gamma,\spec(A))\ge c_\Gamma>0\).  For every fixed
\(r\ge1\) and \(z_1,\ldots,z_r\in\Gamma\),
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{N^2}\sum_{a\ne b}
 \left|
 \bigl((A-z_1I)^{-1}\cdots(A-z_rI)^{-1}\bigr)_{ab}
 \right|^2
 \le\frac{C_{\Gamma,r}}{N}.
\end{equation}
The same estimate holds in expectation when the spectral separation event has
probability \(1-o(N^{-D})\) for every fixed \(D\), with the complement
controlled by a standard resolvent cutoff.

