[Corollary cor:ordinary-lumped-BE-kernel of arXiv:2605.16244, 'Analysis of a twisted Bose--Einstein Markov chain with applications to sampling Catalan st'] Let $\overline K_{\mathrm{BE}}$ and $\overline\pi_{\mathrm{BE}}$ be the transition kernel and stationary distribution of the lumped Bose--Einstein Markov chain on $\mathrm{Comp}_{n,k}$. Then for all $\bm{i},\bm{j}\in\mathrm{Comp}_{n,k}$,
\begin{equation}
 \overline K_{\mathrm{BE}}(\bm{i},\bm{j})
 =
 \sum_{M=(m_{a,b})\in\mathcal{M}(\bm{i},\bm{j})}
 \prod_{a,b=1}^k
 \frac{(1/k)_{(m_{a,b})}}{m_{a,b}!},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 \overline\pi_{\mathrm{BE}}(\bm{i})
 =
 \frac1{\binom{n+k-1}{k-1}}.
\end{equation}

[Lemma l:TransferCyl of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Assume that~$\msR\subset \msS(\mbbI)$. Then,
\[
\mathrm{em}^*u\eqdef u\circ \mathrm{em} \in \msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{} \quad \text{for every} \quad u\in \hCylP{}{0} \,\mathrm{.}
\]
\begin{proof}
Since~$\msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{}$ is an algebra, it suffices to show that~$\mathrm{em}^*u \in \msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{}$ for every~$u\in \hCylP{}{0}$ of the form~$u=(\varphi\otimes f)^\star$.
For every such~$u$, and since~$u\in \hCylP{}{0}$, there exists~$\epsilon=\epsilon_u>0$ such that~$\varphi\in\msR_\epsilon$.
Setting~$n\eqdef \left\lceil1/\epsilon\right\rceil\in {\mathbb N}$, we then have
\begin{equation}
(\varphi\otimes f)^\star\big({\mathrm{em}(\mbfs,\mbfx)}\big)= \sum_{k=1}^\infty s_k\varphi(s_k) f(x_k)= \sum_{k=1}^n s_k\varphi(s_k) f(x_k)
\,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
Since~$\msR\subset \msS(\mbbI)$, the function~$\mbfs\mapsto s_k\varphi(s_k)$ is an element of~$\msS(\mbfT)$ for every~$k$, by continuity (Borel measurability) of the coordinate projections.
Furthermore, since~$f\in\msA$, the function~$\mbfx\mapsto f(x_k)$ is an element of~$\Cyl{}{}$ for every~$k$, for the choice~$A=\left\{k\right\}$.
Thus,~$(\mbfs,\mbfx)\mapsto s_k\varphi(s_k) f(x_k)$ is an element of~$\msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{}$, hence so is~$u\circ \mathrm{em}$ being a finite a sum of functions therein.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma l:intermediate_estimate_pressure_deterministic0 of arXiv:2607.15140, 'On the absence of blow-up in the 3D Navier-Stokes equations with transport noise'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (t:caccioppoli of the paper), for all $\alpha\in (0,1)$,  it holds that  
\begin{align*}
\Big({\mathbf E}\sup_{\alpha I}\Big\|v-\fint_{\alpha B} v\Big\|_{L^2(\alpha B)}^r\Big)^{1/r}
&+
\big({\mathbf E}\|\nabla v\|_{L^2(\alpha Q)}^r\big)^{1/r}\\
&+
