{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e2e7a88f1d03cc5799d54e7b874b4a1a5a6cbc19f9cefadfb8625adf31a85423","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e2/e2e7a88f1d03cc5799d54e7b874b4a1a5a6cbc19f9cefadfb8625adf31a85423.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:0be10f9b110a1d6d0127942d95e4fcdc99ef0cabf88eed85b9919862b47cc35c","bundle_entry_key":"2605-28106-theorem-18-series","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_027","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_027.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2605-28106-theorem-18-series","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: For \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), let \( S_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \xi_k h_k \) and \( R_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \langle h_k, \cdot \rangle h_k \) be the \( n \)th partial sums.
    Then \( S_n \) is a separably valued random element in \( X \) with distribution \( \gamma_n \).
    Independence of \( (\xi_k) \) gives
    \begin{equation*}
        \widehat{\gamma}_n(f)
        = \prod_{k=1}^{n}\exp{\left( -\frac{1}{2}\langle h_k, f \rangle^2 \right)}
        = \exp{\left( -\frac{1}{2}\langle R_nf, f \rangle \right)}
        \qquad \forall f \in X^*.
    \end{equation*}
    Hence \( S_n \) is a Gaussian random element.
