[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2609.00209, 'Exit Times for Brownian Motion and Location Detection'] Let $\mathcal P$ denote our usual parabolic domain and let $u_1^{\mathcal P}(x,y)=\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_{\mathcal P}]$ and $u_2^{\mathcal P}(x,y)=\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_{\mathcal P}^2]$ denote the first two exit time moments of Brownian motion in $\mathcal P$. Then
\begin{align*}
u_1^{\mathcal P}(x,y)&=\frac{1}{2c}(y-cx^2),\\
u_2^{\mathcal P}(x,y)&=\frac{1}{6c}\left(5y-cx^2+\frac{5}{c}\right)u_1^{\mathcal P}(x,y).
\end{align*}

