body:
[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2607.18619, 'Chernoff's Density Is Strongly Log-Concave'] For the density function $g$ in \eqref{eq:M-def} and $\curv, \curvg$ in~\eqref{eq:v-kappa}, 
\begin{equation}
    \curvg(x)>0, \qquad x\in\R, 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \curvg(x)\ge 4x, \qquad x\in\R.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
    \curv(x)>4|x|, \qquad x\in\R, 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
     \inf_{x\in\R}\curv(x)>0.
\end{equation}
Thus Chernoff's density is strongly log-concave. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma le:integrate-one-spin of arXiv:2411.06195, 'Restrictions of some reinforced processes to subgraphs'] Let $1\in\Lambda$ and set $1^c=\Lambda\setminus\{1\}$.
  One has
  \begin{align}

    \int_{H^{2|2}}\mathcal{D}\sigma_1\, e^{\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\left\langle{\sigma_1,\sigma_i}\right\rangle}
    =e^{-\|\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\sigma_i\|},
  \end{align}
  and consequently, 
\begin{align}

  \int_{H^{2|2}}\mathcal{D}\sigma_1\,
    e^{\frac12\sum_{i,j\in\Lambda}W_{ij}(1+\left\langle{\sigma_i,\sigma_j}\right\rangle)}
      =& e^{\frac12\sum_{i,j\in 1^c}W_{ij}(1+\left\langle{\sigma_i,\sigma_j}\right\rangle)
    +\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}-\|\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\sigma_i\|}.
\end{align}

