body:
[Lemma 78 (Stopping, shift, and concatenation formulas) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] Let $L=T-s$.  For $v\in[0,T]$,
\begin{align}
 &Y_v^\eps(\theta_sx)
 -\bigl(Y_{s+(v\wedge L)}^\eps(x)-Y_s^\eps(x)\bigr)
 \notag\\
 &\qquad
 =-(1-e^{-v/\eps})D_s^\eps(x)
 +\one_{\{v\ge L\}}(1-e^{-(v-L)/\eps})D_T^\eps(x).

\end{align}
Furthermore,
\begin{equation}
 Y_t^\eps(r_sx)-(r_sY^\eps(x))_t
 =\one_{\{t>s\}}(1-e^{-(t-s)/\eps})D_s^\eps(x),
\end{equation}
and the delayed path satisfies
\begin{equation}
 (Y^\eps,J^\eps,Q^\eps,\mathsf E^\eps)_t(\lambda_sy)
 =\begin{cases}
  (0,0,0,0),&t\le s,\\
  (Y^\eps,J^\eps,Q^\eps,\mathsf E^\eps)_{t-s}(y),&t>s.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
If $z=x\otimes_sy$, then, for $t\ge s$,
\begin{align}
 J_t^\eps(z)
 &=J_t^\eps(r_sx)+J_t^\eps(\lambda_sy)
   +x_s\otimes y_{t-s}
   -D_s^\eps(x)\otimes\mathcal A_{t-s}^\eps(y),
 \\
 Q_t^\eps(z)
 &=Q_t^\eps(r_sx)+Q_t^\eps(\lambda_sy)
 \notag\\
 &\quad
  +D_s^\eps(x)\otimes\mathcal A_{t-s}^\eps(y)
  +\mathcal A_{t-s}^\eps(y)\otimes D_s^\eps(x).

\end{align}
Finally, if
\[
 \widetilde Y_t^\eps:=
 \begin{cases}
  Y_t^\eps(x),&t\le s,\\
  Y_s^\eps(x)+Y_{t-s}^\eps(y),&t>s,
 \end{cases}
\]
then
\begin{equation}
 Y_t^\eps(z)-\widetilde Y_t^\eps
 =\one_{\{t>s\}}(1-e^{-(t-s)/\eps})D_s^\eps(x).
\end{equation}

[Definition 19 of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] For any $\ka,\eps,t>0$, we let $\mc K_{\ka,\eps}(t)$ denote the integral operator on $L^2(D)$ with kernel
\begin{align}

\mc K_{\ka,\eps}(t;x,y)=p_t(x-y)\mbf E_B\left[\mbf 1_{\{\tau_D(B^{x,y}_t)>t\}}\exp\left(-\ka\int_0^t\xi_\eps\big(B^{x,y}_t(r)\big)\d r\right)\right],\qquad x,y\in D,
\end{align}
where we assume that $B^{x,y}_t$ and $\xi_\eps$ are independent, and we use $\mbf E_B$ to
denote the (conditional) expectation with respect to the randomness in $B$ only.

[Definition 6 (Fredholm determinant) of arXiv:2608.24426, 'Sequential operator learning under dependent data'] Let $\anyoperator\in\Schatten_1(\Hspace)$ be a trace-class operator on a separable Hilbert space $\Hspace$. The Fredholm determinant $\det(\idop + \anyoperator)$ is given by:
    \begin{equation}
        \det(\idop + \anyoperator) = \prod_{i=1}^\infty (1 + \eigval_i(\anyoperator)),
    \end{equation}
    where $\eigval_i(\anyoperator)$ denotes the $i$th eigenvalue of $\anyoperator$, including multiplicities and zeros.

