body:
[Proposition 8 (Endpoint estimates for the weighted Poisson equation) of arXiv:2607.10769, '\fontsize{14}{17}\selectfont\bfseries Sharp Poincar\'e interpolation along Wasserstein geo'] For every $g\in L^2(\mu_0)$ with $\int g\,\d\mu_0=0$, there exists a unique
$u\in H^1(\mu_0)$ satisfying $\int u\,\d\mu_0=0$ and 
\begin{equation}
 -\diver_{\mu_0}(H^{-1}\nabla u)=g
\end{equation}
in the weak sense. Setting $X=H^{-1}\nabla u$ and defining $Y$ by
$Y\circ T=\nabla u$, one also has
\begin{equation}
	-(\diver_{\mu_1}Y)\circ T=g 
\end{equation}
in the weak sense, together with the estimates 
\begin{equation}
\norm{X}_{L^2(\mu_0)}
 \leq\kappa_0^{-1/2}\norm{g}_{L^2(\mu_0)},\qquad
 \norm{Y}_{L^2(\mu_1)}=\norm{\nabla u}_{L^2(\mu_0)}
 \leq\kappa_1^{-1/2}\norm{g}_{L^2(\mu_0)}.
\end{equation}

