[Prop eta_zeta of arXiv:2411.02156, 'Martin boundary of a degenerate Reflected Brownian Motion in a wedge'] For $s \in {\mathbb R}$, we define
\begin{equation}
    \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        \zeta s = -s + 2\mu_2\\
	  \eta s  = -s - 2\mu_1.
    \end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
Then, $x(\zeta s) = x(s)$ and $y(\eta s ) = y(s)$ for all $s \in {\mathbb R}$. Therefore, $\phi_2(x(\zeta s)) = \phi_2(x(s))$ and $\phi_1(y(\eta s)) = \phi_1(y(s))$ in their respective domains of definition. Furthermore, for all $n\in{\mathbb Z}$ and $s\in {\mathbb R}$, we have
\begin{equation}
(\eta \zeta)^n s =s - 2n.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

