{"artifact_digest":"sha256:332f07e80f6160a05233d978ae8a391b628e0ff79ae68131c680e215dd4f8877","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/33/332f07e80f6160a05233d978ae8a391b628e0ff79ae68131c680e215dd4f8877.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:9e8bff32d079c8fb43b30637ca1148d71dbb0e9e48da87cfd29991773d786676","bundle_entry_key":"2606-30411-theorem-6-gaussian-input","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_061","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_061.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-30411-proposition-6-gaussian-input","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: It is clear that for $p=1,2$,
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\Eb \max_{1\leq j\leq p}|G_j|}{\sqrt{\log(2p)}} = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi\log 2}}.
\end{equation*} Therefore, one has
\begin{equation*}
    \inf_{p\geq 1}\frac{\Eb \max_{1\leq j\leq p}|G_j|}{\sqrt{\log(2p)}} \leq \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi\log 2}}=:c_*.
\end{equation*} In fact, one can numerically verify that
\begin{equation*}
    \inf_{1\leq p\leq 8}\frac{\Eb \max_{1\leq j\leq p}|G_j|}{\sqrt{\log(2p)}} =c_*.
\end{equation*}

