[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2607.27082, '2607.27082'] There exists $C<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
\PP(\tau_1^+(Y_n)\le t)\le Ct,
\qquad n\ge1,\quad 0<t\le1.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\PP(\tau_n^+\le tn^r)\le Ct,
\qquad n\ge1,\quad 0<t\le1.
\end{equation}

[Lemma 27 of arXiv:2604.02312, 'A weak transport approach to the Schrödinger--Bass bridge'] The function $C_{\rm stat}^\beta\colon \R^d \times \cP_2(\R^d) \to \R$, defined by
    \begin{align}

        C_{\rm stat}^\beta(x,\eta) &:= \sup_{y \in \R^d} \left\{-q_\beta(x-y) + \inf_{\rho  \in \cP_2(\R^d)}\left\{ H(\rho \,|\, \gamma_y) + W_\beta(\rho,\eta)\right\}\right\} \\

        &= \inf_{\substack{\kappa \in \cP_2(\R^d),\\ \bar \kappa = \bar \eta}} \bigl\{H(\kappa\,|\,\gamma_x) + W_\beta(\kappa,\eta)\bigr\}
    \end{align}
    is a continuous standard weak transport cost and admits a $2$-growth bound. 
    Moreover, $\eta \mapsto C_{\rm stat}^\beta(x,\eta)$ is strictly convex for every $x \in\Rd$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

