[Lemma lem:geometric of arXiv:2411.03984, 'Diffusion of the Random Lorentz Process in a Magnetic Field'] Let $u \in S_1$ be uniformly distributed on the circle and $\alpha \sim 2 \arccos({\tt UNI}[-1,1])$. There exists a constant $C< \infty$ such that for $\varepsilon>0$ small enough, any time $\xi \in (0,\infty)$, and any vector $v \in \R^2$ 
  \begin{align}
    \probab{ \min_{t<\xi} \abs{\cY(t) - v} <\varepsilon} \le C\frac{\varepsilon}{\abs{v}}\xi.
  \end{align}
  where $\{t \mapsto \cY(t)\}$ is the process described above.

