[Proposition 10 (Composition law) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Cut-factorizable maps form a category.  If $\Theta$ and $\Psi$ are
composable cut-factorizable maps, then
\begin{equation}

 \Cost(\Psi\Theta)\le\Cost(\Psi)\Cost(\Theta).
\end{equation}
The identity map has cut cost one.

[Lemma lem: Airy bounds of arXiv:2606.19207, 'Extrema of cooling branching Brownian motion and related Gaussian fields'] There is a constant $C_{\mathrm A}<\infty$ such that, for every $n\geq 1$, $q>0$, and $x\geq 0$,
	\begin{equation}
		\lvert\psi_n^q(x)\rvert\leq C_{\mathrm A}\sqrt{q}\,x.
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, $\alpha_nn^{-2/3}\longrightarrow(3\pi/2)^{2/3}$. Consequently, for every $m>0$,
	\begin{equation}}
		\varrho(m)^2:=\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}
		\exp\{-2(\alpha_n-\alpha_1)m\}<\infty,
		\qquad \varrho(m)\longrightarrow 0\quad(m\to\infty).
	\end{equation}
	Finally, for every $L>0$, there is $C_L>0$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		\psi_1^q(x)\geq C_L\sqrt{q}\,x,
		\qquad x\in[0,Lq^{-1/3}].
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 4 of arXiv:2606.30364, 'Distributional comparison for non-commutative infinitely divisible probability measures'] Let $\circledast \in \{ \ast, \boxplus ,\uplus, \rhd \}$. 

A probability measure $\mu \in \mathcal{P}_{\infty}(\R)$ is said to be \emph{a compound $\circledast$-Poisson distribution with rate $ \lambda \geq 0 $ and jump distribution $\Poiss \in \mathcal{P}_{\infty}(\R)$} if for every integer $ {\ell} \geq 1$ we have that

\begin{align}
\kappa^{\circledast}_{\ell}[\mu] = \lambda \, m_{\ell}\;\![\Poiss] .
\end{align}

