[Theorem thm:asymptotic-LaCroix-coefficients of arXiv:2609.03532, 'Airy limit for the Jack process and topological expansion'] For every $g\in\frac12\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$ and
$0\leq i\leq\lfloor g\rfloor$, the following limit exists and is independent
of the approximating integer sequences in~(equation eq:map-edge-powers of the paper):
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \lim_{N\to\infty}
  N^{2-m-2g}4^{-E_N}
  \operatorname{Map}^{\mathrm{LC}}_{g,i}
  (K_{1,N},\ldots,K_{m,N})
  =
  2C_2^m
  \mathbf B_{m-1+i,\,2g-2i}^{\mathrm{conn}}
  \left[\frac{\mu_+\mathfrak c_2}{2}\boldsymbol k\right].
  }

\end{equation}
In particular, every fixed La Croix coefficient has an explicit finite
nonnegative Brownian integral formula.

[Lemma 31 (Rudin--Shapiro bounds) of arXiv:2608.22342, 'Completely Positive Entropy and Fourier Central Limit Theorems for Stationary Random Measu'] For every $N\geq1$ and $|z|=1$,
\begin{equation}

    \int_Y |P_N(y,z)|^2\,d\nu(y)=N,
    \qquad
    \sup_{y\in Y}|P_N(y,z)|\leq\sqrt{10N}.
\end{equation}

