{"artifact_digest":"sha256:dcbf556c0247b55b26810dbeb04279867a1a2db189fd8e84c92bd50b263e4894","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/dc/dcbf556c0247b55b26810dbeb04279867a1a2db189fd8e84c92bd50b263e4894.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:c55b9fe49878271f3073d1f0e595a4ab75f4b772e83509ccd818cc69f0e22136","bundle_entry_key":"2607-13849-theorem-7-prop-3-1","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_070","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_070.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-13849-prop-7-prop-3-1","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof relies on the verification of \textbf{[T1]} and \textbf{[T2]} in the statement of Theorem~\ref{thm:tightness:D}.
\vskip 4pt

\textit{First Step.}
We first prove that for all $t \in [0,T]$, the family $\{X_t^{(h)}\}_{{h \in T/{\mathbb N}^*}}$ forms a tight sequence of law on $\mathcal{H}^{-1}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S})$. 
As the embedding from $L^2_{\mathrm{Sym}}({\mathbb S})$ 
into $\mathcal{H}^{-1}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S})$ is compact, 
