[Definition of arXiv:2510.01838, 'Shadow and percolation I: discrete landscapes with independence'] Let $X\in \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{Z}^2}$ and $j\in \mathbb{Z}^2$. For $u\in \mathbb{Z}$ we denote by $\alpha_j(u)$ the quantity
    $$\alpha_j(u):= \alpha(u,j)=\sup_{r\geq 1}\frac{X(u+r,j)-X(u,j)}{r}.$$

    Let $u,v\in \mathbb{Z}$ be such that $u<v$, we denote by $\tau^X_j(u,v)$ the quantity
    \begin{equation}
        \tau^X_j(u,v):= \frac{X(v,j)-X(u,j)}{v-u}.
    \end{equation}

[Lemma lemmaJM of arXiv:2604.11212, 'Sofic measures'] A probability measure~$\mu$ on~$A^\mathbb{Z}$ is~$1$-step Markov if and only if
\begin{displaymath}
\hat{\mu}(abw)=\frac{\hat{\mu}(ab)\hat{\mu}(bw)}{\hat{\mu}(b)}
\end{displaymath}
for every~$a,b\in A$ and~$w\in A^*$.

