[Lemma mg_problem_psi of arXiv:2601.04997, 'Fluctuations of the Boundary-Driven Symmetric Zero-Range Process from the NESS'] Let $\mathscr{Y}$ be an $\mathcal{S}'$-valued stochastic process satisfying (equation eq:martingale_problem of the paper) and items i) and ii) of Proposition~(prop:uniqueness_neg of the paper) for each $H\in\mathcal{S}$. Fix $\beta\in(0, 1)$ and $\alpha>\frac{1}{1-\beta}$. For $\psi\in\{\psi_{\alpha, \beta}, \tilde\psi_{\alpha, \beta}\}$, the process $\{\mathscr{\tilde M}_t(\psi), t\in[0, T]\}$ defined via 
\begin{equation}
    \mathscr{\tilde M}_t(\psi):=\mathscr{Y}_t(\psi)-\mathscr{Y}_0(\psi)-\int_0^t\mathscr{Y}_s(\mathfrak{A}_\theta \psi)\de s
\end{equation}
is a martingale with continuous trajectories and with quadratic variation given by
\begin{equation}
    \scal{\mathscr{\tilde M}(\psi)}_t=t\|\partial_u \psi\|_{L^{2, \theta}}^2.
\end{equation}
Moreover, the process $\{\mathscr{Y}_t(\psi), t\in[0, T]\}$ has continuous trajectories.

