[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2605.30616, 'Brownian motion: the hyperbolic number setting'] Let $p\wedge q$ and $|p-q|_{\mathbb H}$ be defined by \eqref{min-sup} and \eqref{p-q} respectively. The function 
\[
p\wedge q,\quad p,q\in\mathbb{H}_{+}
\]
is positive definite on $\mathbb{H}_{+}$ and the function
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2}\left(|p|_{\mathbb{H}}+|q|_{\mathbb{H}}-|p-q|_{\mathbb{H}}\right),\quad p,q\in\mathbb{H}
\end{equation}
is positive definite on $\mathbb{H}$.

[Theorem MainThm: LowerBound_Smaller_d of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $d \ge 1$. Assume $Q$ is a symmetric, translation-invariant, and heavy-tailed transition matrix on $\mathbb Z^d$ with exponent $\alpha \in (0, d)$. Consider the frog model on \( \mathbb Z^d \) equipped with $Q$. Fix any particle lifespan \( \ell \in \mathbb{N} \) and particle density \( \lambda > 0 \). Then, there exists a positive constant $c = c(Q, \lambda, \ell)$ such that $\mathbb P$-almost surely $\mathcal{A}(c(\log L)^\Delta) \subseteq B(L)$ for all large $L$ and 
\begin{align*}
	\lim_{L \rightarrow \infty} \frac{|\mathcal{A}(c(\log L)^\Delta)|}{L^d} = 0,
\end{align*}
where $B(L) := [-L, L]^d \cap \mathbb Z^d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

