[Definition 7 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Region] 
Given $M \subset M_0$ and $R \subset \mathbf{R}^2$. $R$ is called a \textit{region} on $M$ if it is a union of triangles in $Tri(M)$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
\exists U \subset Tri(M) \text{ such that } R=\bigcup_{abc \in U}  abc  \subset \mathbf{R}^2.
\end{equation*}
$REG(M)$ denotes the collection of all regions on $M$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
REG(M):=\{ \bigcup_{abc \in U}  abc  \subset \mathbf{R} \ |\  U \subset Tri(M) \}.
\end{equation*}
 A collection of regions is called a \textit{region complex}. 
 Given a region complex $R=\{r_1,\ r_2,\ \ldots,\ r_k\} \subset REG(M)$ ($k \in \mathbf{N}$). $|R|$ denotes the union of all regions in $R$, i.e., 
\begin{equation*}
|R|:=r_1 \cup r_2 \cup \cdots \cup r_k \quad \in REG(M).
\end{equation*}

