[Corollary cor:suplinpa of arXiv:2411.18749, 'A star is born: Explosive Crump--Mode--Jagers branching processes'] Let $(T_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a preferential attachment tree with fitness function $f$ that satisfies Assumption~(equation eq:minfass of the paper). Let $(\lambda_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}\subset [0,\infty)$ be increasing and tend to infinity with $n$, such that there exists $N\in\mathbb{N}$ with $\lambda_n< f(i,w^*)$ for all $i\geq n\geq N$. If
	\begin{equation} 
	\sum_{n=N}^{\infty} \bigg(\prod_{i=n}^\infty \frac{f(i,w^*)}{f(i,w^*)-\lambda_n}\bigg)\E{\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}\frac{f(i,W)}{f(i,W) + \lambda_n}} < \infty,
	\end{equation} 
	then the tree $T_{\infty}$ contains a unique node of infinite degree, and no infinite path, almost surely. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2607.02019, 'Critical branching processes in random environment with immigration and an application to '] In the critical randomised reproducing graph with $\beta>0$ and $\alpha=\delta$,
\begin{equation*}
	\frac{D_n}{\gamma(1-\gamma)n}\;\Rightarrow\;D
\end{equation*}
where $D\sim\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\beta}{\gamma(1-\gamma)},1\right)$.
Consequently,
\begin{equation*}
	p_d^{(n)}\to 0
\end{equation*}
for each fixed $d$.

[Lemma lem:It_asymp-2 of arXiv:2605.15545, 'Crossover from subcritical to critical decay: \\ random walk, self-avoiding walk, percolat'] Let $d \ge 1$, $\varepsilon \in (0,1] $, and denote
$\zeta_0 = \zeta \wedge \frac {d \wedge 2}2$.
For any $t \ge \varepsilon^{-1} \vee \varepsilon^{-(2+\zeta_0)/\zeta_0}$ and any $x\in {\mathbb Z^d}$, we have
\begin{equation}  
\bigabs{ I_{t}(x) - \hat g(0) \rho_t(x; \eta, \Lambda) }
\le C_1 \biggl[ e^{-c/\varepsilon}
	+ \frac{1}{  (\sqrt \varepsilon )^{d+4} (\sqrt{ \varepsilon t})^{\zeta_0}} \biggr]
	\frac 1 {(\sqrt t)^d}
	\biggl( \frac{ \sqrt t } { \abs{ x - \integer{t \eta} } } \biggr)^n
	\qquad (n = 0,1,2)
\end{equation}
(here $n=0$ means the factor is absent),
with $c = \frac 1 4 K_{\mathrm{IR}} $ and $C_1$ depending only on $n, d, \zeta, M, K_{\mathrm{IR}}$.

