[Theorem eqn:exponential mixing 2 of arXiv:2606.29352, 'Exponential Mixing for 2D Stochastic Damped Euler Equation Driven by Bounded Noise'] Let $\gamma>0$. There exists $\epsilon(\gamma)>0$ such that there exists a unique invariant measure $\mu^\epsilon$ on $E$ for Eq. ((eqn:Euler 2 of the paper)). Moreover, for any $0<\epsilon<\epsilon(\gamma)$, $x \in E$ and $\phi \in \text{Lip}_b(E)$,
    \begin{equation}
        |P_i^\epsilon\phi(x)-\int_{E}\phi\;d\mu^\epsilon\vert \leq c(x)e^{-c(\gamma)i}\|\phi\Vert_{\text{Lip}},
    \end{equation}
    where $c(x)$, $c(\gamma)>0$ depend on $x$ and $\gamma$ respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition defi-v of arXiv:2604.24914, 'SPDEs with time-independent\\ L\'evy colored noise'] {\rm
The process $\{v(t,x);t\geq 0,x\in \mathbb{R}^d\}$ defined by
\begin{equation}

v(t,x)=\int_0^t \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}G_{t-s}(x-y) X(dy)ds
\end{equation}
is called a {\em random-field solution} of equation (equation lin-eq of the paper), provided that the integral is well-defined. }

[Lemma eq:Lcal_hatH0 of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] The Laplace transform of $\hat{H}^2$ under the Palm probability measure $\mathbb{P}^0_{\mathcal V }$ is 

\begin{equation}
    {\mathcal L }^0_{\hat{H}^2}(\gamma)= \mathbb{E}^0_{\mathcal V }\left[e^{-\gamma \hat{H}^2}\right]=\left(1+\frac{\gamma}{\lambda \pi}\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}},

\end{equation} 

for any $\gamma>0$. The squared handover-distance $\hat{H}^2$ follows a Gamma distribution with parameters $\left(\frac{3}{2}, \frac{1}{\lambda\pi}\right)$.   Moreover, the handover distance $\hat{H}$ follows a Nakagami distribution with parameters $\left(\frac{3}{2}, \frac{3}{2\lambda\pi}\right)$.

