[Proposition prop:zeta as ellips of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $\mathcal{X}\randin \mathscr{F}\subset C^\infty(M)$ be an admissible centered Gaussian random field with covariance $\Sigma$. Endow $\mathscr{F}$ with the scalar product induced by $\Sigma$. Then, for all $x\in M$, we have
  \begin{equation}
    \zeta_\mathcal{X}(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi \sqrt{\mathrm{K}(x)}}(D_x\circ \pi_x)(B_{\Sigma}^\circ)
  \end{equation}
  where $\pi_x:\mathscr{F}\to \mathscr{F}_x$ is the orthogonal projection and where recall that $B_\Sigma^\circ$ denotes the dual of the unit ball of the quadratic form $\Sigma$.

