[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2604.14752, 'Strong and weak rates of convergence in the Smoluchowski--Kramers approximation for stocha'] For all $\alpha\in\R$, all $u\in H^\alpha$ and all $t\ge 0$, one has
\begin{equation}
\|e^{-t\IL}u\|_{H^\alpha}\le e^{-t\lambda_1}\|u\|_{H^\alpha}.
\end{equation}
In addition, the following smoothing property is satisfied: for all $\alpha\ge 0$, there exists $C_\alpha\in(0,\infty)$ such that for all $t>0$ and all $u\in H$ one has
\begin{equation}
\|e^{-t\IL}u\|_{H^\alpha}\le C_\alpha t^{-\frac{\alpha}{2}}\|u\|_H.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:antipode of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] For a weakly geometric path $X$ with reversal $\overleftarrow X$ and every word $w=(i_1\cdots i_k)$,
\begin{equation}
\inner{\mathrm{Sig}(\overleftarrow X)}{i_1\cdots i_k}=(-1)^k\,\inner{\mathrm{Sig}(X)}{i_k\cdots i_1}.
\end{equation}

