[Lemma l1 of arXiv:2411.19433, 'Singular mean-field backward stochastic Volterra integral equations in infinite dimensiona'] Assume that condition $(A_{2})$ holds. Then, for any \( \Psi(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}_T}(\delta, b; H) \), equation (equation f3.4 of the paper) has a unique adapted solution 

\[
(\lambda(t, \cdot), \mu(t, \cdot)) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(; C([r, b]; H)) \times L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(r, b; L^2_0)
\]

for almost every \( t \in [\delta, b] \). Moreover, if \( \bar{h} \) also satisfies $(A_{2})$, \( \bar{\Psi}(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}_b}(\delta, b; H) \), and \begin{align*} 
(\bar{\lambda}(t, \cdot), \bar{\mu}(t, \cdot)) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(; C([r, b]; H)) \times L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(r, b; L^2_0) \end{align*} 

is the unique adapted solution to (equation f3.4 of the paper) when \( (h, \Psi) \) is replaced by \( (\bar{h}, \bar{\Psi}) \). Then we have the estimate:

\begin{align}
&\quad \mathbb{E} \bigg\{ \sup_{\tau \in [r, b]} |\lambda(t, \tau) - \bar{\lambda}(t, \tau)|_H^2 + \int_{\tau}^{b} |\mu(t, s) - \bar{\mu}(t, s)|_{L^2_0}^2 \, ds \bigg\} \nonumber\\
&\leq C \, \mathbb{E} \bigg\{ |\Psi(t) - \bar{\Psi}(t)|_H^2 + \left( \int_{\tau}^{b} \vert \eta(t, s, \mu(t, s), \mathbb{E}[\mu(t, s)]) - \bar{\eta}(t, s, \bar{\mu}(t, s), \mathbb{E}[\bar{\mu}(t, s)])\vert_{H} \, ds \right)^2 \bigg\},
\end{align}

for almost every \( t \in [\delta, b] \).

[Theorem t:joint.sum.max.clique of arXiv:2601.12677, 'Stable and Fréchet limit theorem for subgraph functionals in the hyperbolic random geometr'] Let $\frac{2m-3}{2m-2}<\alpha<1$. Then, as $n\to\infty$, 
\begin{align}
&\left( \frac{1}{b_{m,n}} \bigg( \sum_{i=1}^{[N_n\cdot\,]}\mathcal C_{m,n}(X_i) - [N_n\cdot\,]  \mathbb{E} [\mathcal C_{m,n}(X_1)] \bigg), \ \frac{1}{b_{m,n}}\bigvee_{i=1}^{[N_n \cdot \,]} \mathcal C_{m,n}(X_i)\right)  \Rightarrow (S_{2\alpha}(\cdot),\, Y_{2\alpha}(\cdot)),  
\end{align}
in the space $D\big([0,1], {\mathbb R} \times [0,\infty)\big)$, where the hybrid characteristic-distribution function is given in (equation e:hybrid1 of the paper) with $k=2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

