[Proposition prop:high lln of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $\mu=\mu_n$ be such that $c_n=\mu/n$ converges to some $c\in(0,\infty]$ and assume that $\delta_n$ admits a limit $\delta$ (possibly infinite).
    Let $\mathbf{T}_n$ be a $\mu$-height-biased tree of size $n$.
    Then, $h(\mathbf T_n)-m_{\mu/n}+2$ converges in distribution to a Bernoulli random variable with parameter
    \begin{align*}
        p_{c,\delta}=\frac{m_c\tan^2\left(\frac{\pi}{m_c+1}\right)e^{-\delta}}{(m_c+1)\tan^2\left(\frac{\pi}{m_c}\right)+m_c\tan^2\left(\frac{\pi}{m_c+1}\right)e^{-\delta}}\,.
    \end{align*}

