[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2607.01629, 'Quadratic fluctuations of speed-change Kawasaki dynamics'] \cite[Theorem~1]{fun96}
		The process $(\Y_t^N)_{t \in [0,T]}$ converges weakly as $N\to\infty$ in the space $D\left([0,T], \S' \left(\Td\right)\right)$. The limit is the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process:
		\begin{equation}
			\d \Y_t=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf D(\rho)\partial^2 \Y_t\right)\,\d t+\sqrt{\cc(\rho)}\nabla\cdot\d\omega(t),
		\end{equation}
		where $\partial^2\Y=\{\partial_{i}\partial_{j}\Y\}_{1\leq i, j\leq d}$ and $\omega=\{\omega_i(t)\}_{1\leq i\leq d}$ is the $d$-dimensional space-time white noise. Moreover, the initial data $\Y_0$ is a white noise with intensity $\chi(\rho)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

