[Definition 4 of arXiv:2506.08910, 'Critical points of random polynomials and finite free cumulants'] Let $p$ be a degree $N$ polynomial and let $P$ be a formal power series such that $P\left(\frac{\dd}{\dd x}\right)x^{N}=p(x)$. Then, the finite $R$-transform of $p$ is the truncated formal power series \begin{equation}
         R_{p}(s):=-\frac{P'(Ns)}{P(Ns)}\mod s^{N}.
     \end{equation}

[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2609.02842, 'Global Well-posedness and Asymptotic Analysis of a Damped Nonlinear Wave Equation with a C'] Let $\vartheta$ be the unique strong solution of problem  \eqref{asym1}. Define 
			\begin{equation}
				\mathcal{X}:=H_0^1(\varOmega)\times L^2(\varOmega),
				\ 

				\mathcal{U}_0:=(\vartheta _0,\vartheta_1). 
			\end{equation}
			Then the orbit
			\begin{equation}
				\mathcal{O}(\vartheta_0,\vartheta_1)
				=
				\left\{
				(\vartheta (t),\vartheta_t(t)):t\geq0
				\right\}
			\end{equation}
			is precompact in $\mathcal{X},$ that is, $\overline{\mathcal{O}(\vartheta_0,\vartheta_1)}^{\mathcal{X}}$ is compact in $\mathcal{X}$.

