[Lemma lem:h:magnetization of arXiv:2411.14111, 'Stochastic processes on preferential attachment models'] Let $\Tbold$ be a multi-type branching process with a general type-space $\Sbold$, and assume that $\Tbold$ is almost surely locally finite. Furthermore, label the nodes of $\Tbold$ using the Ulam-Harris notation. Consider a distributional functional $\h$ as a fixed point solution to the following recursion:
	\begin{equation}
		\h^{(\ell+1)}(\omega) = B + \sum\limits_{i=1}^{D_{\ell}(\omega)} \arctanh\Big( \tanh(\beta)\tanh\big( \h^{(\ell)}(\omega i) \big) \Big),
	\end{equation}
	where $\big(D_{\ell}(\omega)\big)_{\ell\geq1}$ is a sequence of $D(\omega)$, the degree of the node $\omega$ in $\Tbold$, for some $B > 0$ and for all $\omega \in \Sbold$ and $\ell \geq 0$. Then, denoting by $\magn{\omega\to}$ the root magnetisation of $\Tbold$, rooted at $\omega$ under the inverse critical temperature $\beta$ and external magnetic field $B > 0$, for all $\omega \in \Sbold$, we have
	\begin{equation}
		\h(\omega) = \magn{\omega\to},\quad \text{a.s.}
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 25 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Consider the Young-It\^o SDE as below
\begin{equation}
\dd x_t =g(x_t) \dd t+ f(t, x_t) \dd B^H_t + \sigma(x_t) \dd W_t,\ x_0 = x.
\end{equation}
Here, the integral with respect to fractional Brownian motion \(B^H\) is in the pathwise Young integrals by Wiener integrals that introduced in Section 2. The integral with respect to BM \(W\) is in the It\^o sense.
Assume \(f\) is of \(BC^2\), \(\sigma\) and \(g\) are both Lipschitz continuous and linear growth with respect to \(x\). Then, for any function \(\Psi\in C_b^2\), 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Psi(x_t)&=\Psi(x_0)+\int_{0}^{t}D_x\Psi(x_r)g(x_r)\dd r+\int_{0}^{t}D_x\Psi(x_r)f(r, x_r)\dd B_r^H\\
&+\int_{0}^{t}D_x\Psi(x_r)\sigma(x_r)\dd W_r+\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{t}D^2_{xx}\Psi(x_r)a(x_r)\dd r
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
holds. Here, let \(a:=\sigma\sigma^*\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary general-lobes of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Assume that $Q$ is continuous and strictly increasing
on $[0,1]$. Assume that the zeros of $H_{F}$ on $(0,\tfrac{1}{2})$ are 
\begin{equation}
0=\xi _{0}<\xi _{1}<\cdots <\xi _{m}<\xi _{m+1}=\frac{1}{2},  
\end{equation}
and define the lobe areas 
\begin{equation}
A_{k}(F)=\int_{\xi _{k-1}}^{\xi _{k}}|H_{F}(u)|\,du,\qquad k=1,\dots ,m+1.

\end{equation}
If 
\begin{equation}
A_{1}(F)\geq A_{2}(F)\geq \cdots \geq A_{m+1}(F),  
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{equation}
Z(D_{0})<Z(H_{F}).  
\end{equation}

