1 <= k <= m

[Theorem lime of arXiv:2412.04013, 'Rate estimates for total variation distance with applications\thanks{The author gratefully'] Assume that the characteristic function $\phi$ of $\varepsilon_{0}$
satisfies
\begin{equation}
|\phi(u)|\leq C_{\flat}(1+|u|)^{-d-\gamma}
\end{equation} for some $\gamma, C_{\flat}>0$. Then 
$$
d_{TV}(\mathcal{L}(X_{n}),\mu_{*})\leq C'\varrho^{n},\ n\in\mathbb{N}.
$$	
for suitable $C'>0$, $\varrho<1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem 15 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Assume Hypothesis \ref{hyplin}. Then, there exist some $\delta_1 \in (0, \;1]$ and some function $g: \mathbb{N} \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ satisfying $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} g(n)=0$, such that
	\begin{align} 
\forall \alpha,\beta \in S, \; \forall n \in \mathbb{N}_+, \; \forall	0 \leq s \leq t \leq T, \quad	\mathbb{E}_{\nu_{\rho}} \big[ \big\{ \mcb{B}_t^{n, \alpha , \beta}(H) - \mcb{B}_s^{n, \alpha , \beta}(H) \big\}^{2} \big] \leq  C (t-s)^{1 + \delta_1} g(n). 
	\end{align}
	In the last line, $C$ is some constant independent of $n$, $s$ and $t$.

	In particular, the sequence of stochastic processes $\big\{ \big( \mcb{B}_t^{n}(H) \; \big)_{0 \leq t \leq T}, \; n \in \mathbb{N}_+ \big\}$ is tight with respect to the uniform topology of $\mathcal{C} \big( [0, T], \mathbb{R} \big)$, due to the Kolmogorov-Centsov Theorem. Moreover, any of its limit points is identically equal to zero.

[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2606.30938, 'Poissonian potential measures for refracted-reflected L\'evy processes'] Let $p,q \geq 0$ and $\lambda,\gamma> 0$. Then, for $x,b \in [0,a]$, we have that 
			\begin{align}
				\E_x \Bigl( \ee^{-q T_{a,U}^{+,\lambda} - p \int_0^{T_{a,U}^{+,\lambda}} 1_{\left\{U_s<b\right\}} \mathrm{d} s} \; \1_{\{T_{a,U}^{+,\lambda} < T_{b,U}^{-,\gamma}\}}\Bigr) &=  \frac{\overline{z}_b^{(q+\gamma_p, -\gamma_p)}(x)}{\varphi_{q+\lambda} \int^\infty_0 \ee^{-\varphi_{q+\lambda}y}\overline{z}_b^{(q+\gamma_p, -\gamma_p)}(y+a) \dd y},  

			\end{align} 
			where $\overline{z}_b^{(\cdot, \cdot)}$ is defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:SecondGenFunc-RefractedZTilde1}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Cor uniqueness-maps-to-O(n) of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $f_1,f_2\colon {\textup{O}}(m)\to {\textup{O}}(n)$ be homomorphisms. Assume that for every block diagonal matrix
    \begin{equation}
        A=\begin{pmatrix}
            A_1\\
            &\ddots\\
            &&A_r
        \end{pmatrix}
    \end{equation}
    where $A_i\in {\textup{O}}(m_i)$, $1\le m_i\le 2$, $m_1+\cdots+m_r=m$, the two matrices $f_1(A)$ and $f_2(A)$ are conjugate in ${\textup{GL}}(n,{\mathbb{C}})$. Then $f_1$ and $f_2$ are homotopic in $\textup{Glo}$.
    \begin{proof}
        By elementary linear algebra, every orthogonal matrix is conjugate to a block matrix of the form $((eq:orth-normal-form of the paper))$. As any homomorphism sends conjugate elements to conjugate elements, the claim then follows from the previous lemma.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

