{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1f5a0603062f522c671cadf5bfb3cc685bb981cd1a28900bf23b770d6c6ceb7d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/1f/1f5a0603062f522c671cadf5bfb3cc685bb981cd1a28900bf23b770d6c6ceb7d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:7a645114716b25df89d275f6af642c359f05cb3609689991e958f7de7f202578","bundle_entry_key":"2605-13727-lemma-21-le-conv-to-solution","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_031","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_031.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2605-13727-lemma-21-le-conv-to-solution","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: It suffices to establish that there exists a process $X \in D_H$ such that the following convergences hold:
\[\Lambda(X)\stackrel{{\rm(i)}}{=}\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\Lambda(X_n)\stackrel{{\rm(ii)}}{=}\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}X_n\stackrel{{\rm(iii)}}{=}X,\]
where the limits are  in the topology of uniform convergence in probability on compact intervals. The fact that ${\rm(ii)}$ holds is a direct consequence of our assumptions, provided at least one of the two limits exists. Hence, it remains only to establish that there exists a process $X \in D_H$ such that ${\rm(i)}$ and ${\rm(iii)}$ both hold.

