[Lemma C(a)-def-2 of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] With $f, \phi, u_\phi,  C(f, \alpha)$ given as in Lemma (C(a) definition of the paper), it holds that, as $t\to \infty$\footnote{The range of integration for (equation second-def-C(a) of the paper) in [Lemma 9.10-[Bovier_book]] is given to be $(0,\infty)$. However, since $u_{\phi}(t, \sqrt{2}t + x) \to 0$ as $t\to \infty$ pointwise in $x$ (see Lemma (when-does-u-go-to-zero of the paper)), one can easily change the range of integration to $(-\infty, \infty)$ by invoking the dominated convergence theorem.},
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \int_{-\infty}^\infty u_\phi(t, \sqrt{2}t + z) e^{(\sqrt{2}+ \alpha)z - \alpha^2t /2} dz \to C(f, \alpha).     
\end{equation}
In particular, for $\lambda >\sqrt{2}$, $\mu =\lambda + \sqrt{\lambda^2-2}$, and $\alpha(\lambda) := \mu -\sqrt{2}$, by substituting $ z = (\lambda -\sqrt{2})t - x$, 
\begin{equation}
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} u_{\phi}(t, \lambda t -x)e^{-\mu x} dx \to C(f, \alpha(\lambda)). 
\end{equation}
Also, for any fixed $x \in \mathbb{R}$ (see equation (9.50) of [Bovier_book]), 
\begin{equation}
    C(f(x + \cdot), \alpha(\lambda)) = e^{\mu x}C(f, \alpha(\lambda)).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 5 (Common-shape asymptotic relative efficiency) of arXiv:2609.00774, 'Semi-Supervised Classification with Informative Missing Labels in Weibull Mixture Models'] Assume the conditions of \Cref{thm:risk-hessian}. Under a common-shape two-component Weibull mixture, suppose $AB<0$, so that Bayes' rule has the unique simple decision boundary $y_0$. For the $\mathrm{full}$ estimator relative to the estimator based on the completely classified sample,
\begin{equation}
\ARE_{\mathrm{full}:\mathrm{CC}}^{\mathrm{C}}
=
\frac{
g_C(y_0)^\top
\J_{\mathrm{CC},C}^{-1}
g_C(y_0)
}{
g_C(y_0)^\top
\J_{\mathrm{full},C}^{-1}
g_C(y_0)
}.

\end{equation}

