{"artifact_digest":"sha256:b384d616ec1fb7f10cd60bb341eae0e44924a8dbcaf12fa7644495331c3f429d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/b3/b384d616ec1fb7f10cd60bb341eae0e44924a8dbcaf12fa7644495331c3f429d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:448b8540b6fbc71c7dc247af5c075632d75338d7659c9c40f9a683136fcbd175","bundle_entry_key":"2605-00997-lemma-0-lemma-0","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_024","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_024.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2605-00997-lemma-0-lemma-0","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $P(s) = \sum_{n=0}^\infty p_n s^n$ denote the generating function of the sequence $p_n$. For convenience set $a_n = \mathbb{P}(S_n >0)$, $h_n = \mathbb{P}(S_n = 0)$. Let $A(s) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n s^n$, $H(s) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty h_n s^n$ denote the corresponding generating functions of the sequences $a_n$ and $h_n$. By Theorem 1 of [12] for $|s|<1$ we have
\begin{equation}
P(s) =\exp\left({\int_0^s \frac{A(\sigma)}{\sigma}\,d\sigma}\right).
\end{equation}
