[Definition defn:hDeps of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Take any $\epsilon$ with $\hat{C}^{-1}N^{-2/3}<\epsilon<\hat{C}$ for a universal constant $\hat{C}>0$; any $C, c$ can depend on $\hat{C}$. We let $\mathscr B_\epsilon=\mathscr B_\epsilon[i_1,\ldots,i_m]$ be the set of all walks $\vec r$ of $(\hat{\mathfrak D}_{i_m}^{N_m})^{k_m} \cdots (\hat{\mathfrak D}_{i_1}^{N_1})^{k_1}$, with the following requirements:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $J_\mathrm {VI.1}=J_\mathrm {VI.2}=J_\mathrm {VI.3}=J_\mathrm C=\emptyset$.
    \item For each $j\in J_\mathrm I\cup J_\mathrm {II}\cup J_\mathrm {IV}$, $\vartheta_j\wedge \dot\vartheta_j > \epsilon N^{2/3}$.
\end{itemize}

[Proposition T rho eta of arXiv:2605.13479, '\LARGE Dean-Kawasaki Equation with Biot-Savart and Keller-Segel Interactions: Existence an'] Under the same setting as in Proposition (entropy estimate of the paper) and for every $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$ fixed, the laws of $\left\{\rho^{\gamma, \eta}\right\}_{\eta ,\gamma \in(0,1)}$ are tight on $L^1_{[0,T]}L^1_{\mathbb{T}^2}$ with the strong topology. Furthermore, for any $\alpha \in(0,1 / 2)$ and $\psi \in \mathrm{C}_c^{\infty}(\mathbb{T}^2\times(0, \infty))$, the family of laws of the martingales $(M^{\gamma,\eta})_{\eta,\gamma\in(0,1)}$ and $(M^{\gamma,\eta}_{\text{entropy}})_{\eta,\gamma\in(0,1)}$ defined by
 \begin{align}
 M_t^{\gamma,\eta}:=\sqrt{\varepsilon}\int_0^t \int_{\mathbb{T}^2} \psi\left(x, \rho^{\gamma, \eta}\right) \nabla \cdot\left[s_{\eta}\left(\rho^{\gamma, \eta}\right) \xi_{K}\right],  
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
M^{\gamma,\eta}_{\text{entropy},t}:=\sqrt{\varepsilon}\int_0^t\int_{\mathbb{T}^2}
\frac{s_{\eta}(\rho^{\gamma, \eta})}
{\sqrt{\rho^{\gamma, \eta}}}\nabla\sqrt{\rho^{\gamma, \eta}}\cdot\xi_{K}	
\end{align}

is tight on $\mathcal{C}_{[0, T]}\mathbb{R}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition Prop: recursive form remainder of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] For any forest $w=\tau_1\cdots\tau_k\in{\mathcal F}$ and any rooted tree $\tau_0\in{\mathcal T}$ we have
		\begin{equation}
			\sum_{i=1}^k\frac{1}{\pi(\tau_i; w)}H_{z_1z_2}(w\setminus\tau_i;\tau_0) \, \Upsilon_{\tau_i}(z_1)-\frac{1}{\pi(w)}\Upsilon_{w\curvearrowright\tau_0}(z_1)=H_{z_1z_2}(w;\tau_0)\cdot z^{[1]}\,,
\end{equation}
		where $\pi(\tau_i;w)$ is the number of times the tree $\tau_i$ appears in the forest $w$ and $w\setminus\tau_i$ is the forest obtained by removing an instance of $\tau_i$ in $w$, see Definition (Def: symmetry factor of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

