{"artifact_digest":"sha256:14e79824935f2d8123ba57649af20c162a62901ad1966597dbec4239c7ae1fc4","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/14/14e79824935f2d8123ba57649af20c162a62901ad1966597dbec4239c7ae1fc4.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:9092a19af37ab2e515f5d91740f408e3e2c3defd899b74006a4c8e2b9ed94ef3","bundle_entry_key":"2607-04467-theorem-14-renewal-monotonicity","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_064","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_064.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-04467-theorem-14-renewal-monotonicity","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The renewal generating function is
\begin{equation}
F(z)=\sum_{n\ge0}C(n)z^n=(1-K(z))^{-1}=\frac1{\psi(1-z)},\qquad |z|<1.
\end{equation}
For \(\psi\in\SBF\), the potential measure of the corresponding subordinator
has Laplace transform \(1/\psi\), and Proposition~\ref{prop:standard-reps}
gives
\begin{equation}
\frac1{\psi(\lambda)}
=b+\int_0^\infty e^{-\lambda t}u(t)\,dt,\qquad b\ge0,
\end{equation}
