body:
[Proposition gapprocess of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] For $\beta=0$, the gap process of the averaging process is a Markov process on $\Bbb Z$ whose transitions go as follows. Suppose that it sits at $x\in \mathbb{Z}$:
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item  if  $x\neq 0, \pm 1$, it jumps to $x\pm1$ with rate $1$;
        \item  if $x=0$,  it jumps to $1$ with rate $1/2$, and to $-1$ with rate $1/2$;
        \item  if $x=\pm 1$, it jumps to $\mp 1$ with rate $1/4$, to $0$ with rate $1/2$, and to $\pm 2$ with rate $1$.
    \end{enumerate}

[Lemma lem:four_point_Gladkov of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] There exists a universal constant $C$ such that the inequality
\begin{multline*}
  \mathbb P_{\beta,r}(x,y,z,w \text{ \emph{all connected}})^2 \leq C \max\{ \mathbb P_{\beta,r} (x \leftrightarrow y \leftrightarrow z)\mathbb P_{\beta,r} (z \leftrightarrow w)
  \mathbb P_{\beta,r} (x \leftrightarrow y \leftrightarrow w),\\
   \mathbb P_{\beta,r} (x \leftrightarrow y \leftrightarrow z)\mathbb P_{\beta,r} (y \leftrightarrow w)
  \mathbb P_{\beta,r} (x \leftrightarrow z \leftrightarrow w)
  \}
\end{multline*}
holds for all $x,y,z,w\in \mathbb Z^d$ and $\beta,r\geq 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma prob-nontree-small of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Let $F$ be an induced event and let $R$ be chosen independently and uniformly at random from $[n]$. We have	
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb P(R\in \mathcal{C}_n\mid F)=O\left(\frac{|F|}{n}\right).
	\end{align}

