body:
Let X = A union B where A and B are both CLOSED in X (or both open, or more generally a locally finite closed cover). Let f : A -> Y and g : B -> Y be continuous and agree on A intersect B. Then the combined function h : X -> Y defined by h = f on A and h = g on B is well defined and continuous. The closedness (or openness, or local finiteness) of the pieces is essential: for an arbitrary cover by two sets the conclusion is false, as the cover of R by the rationals and the irrationals shows, where two constant functions paste to a nowhere continuous function.
The pieces must be finitely many closed sets, or arbitrarily many open sets, or a locally finite closed family;
and the functions must AGREE on the overlap, which is what makes h well defined.

[Proposition proposition_asymptotic_expansion_G_H of arXiv:2609.00973, 'The Vlasov-Maxwell point charge system : study of the spherically symmetric case'] Let $G_a$ be the function defined in (equation equation_def_G_a of the paper) and $H_a$ its inverse. The following estimates hold 
    \begin{equation}

        \begin{array}{r}
             \displaystyle x-1\leq G_a(x)\leq2x,\quad 1\leq x<+\infty,  \\
            \displaystyle \frac{x}{2}\leq H_a(x)\leq x+1,\quad 0\leq x<+\infty.
        \end{array}
    \end{equation}
    The derivatives of $G_a$ and $H_a$ satisfy
    \begin{equation}

        \begin{array}{ll}
            \displaystyle  G_a'(x)=\frac{x+\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}}{\sqrt{x^2-1}}, & \qquad\displaystyle\partial_a G_a(x)=\partial_a\left(\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}\right)\arcosh(x),\vspace{5pt}\\
            \displaystyle  H_a'(x)=\frac{\sqrt{H_a(x)^2-1}}{H_a(x)+\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}}, & \displaystyle\qquad\partial_a H_a(x)=-\partial_a\left(\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}\right)\arcosh(H_a(x))H_a'(x).
            \end{array}
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, $H_a$ and $G_a$ satisfy the following asymptotic expansions
    \begin{equation}

    \begin{array}{l}
        \displaystyle G_a(y)=y+\left(\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}\right)\log(2y) +O_{y\rightarrow +\infty}\left(\frac{1}{y}\right) ,\vspace{4pt}\\
        \displaystyle H_a(x)=x-\left(\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}\right)\log(2x)+\left(\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}\right)^2\frac{\log(2x)}{2x}+O_{x\rightarrow +\infty}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right),
        \end{array}
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation*}
        G_a(1+h)=\sqrt{2}\left(1+\kappa-\frac{1}{a^0p}\right)\sqrt{h}+O_{h\rightarrow 0}\left(h^\frac{3}{2}\right),\qquad H_a(h)=1+\left(\frac{a^0\sqrt{m^2+\ell a^2}}{m^2+a^0\sqrt{m^2+\ell a^2}}\right)^2\frac{h^2}{2}+O_{h\rightarrow0}(h^4).
    \end{equation*}
    Here, the remainder terms are all uniform in $a,\ell$.

