body:
[Theorem cor:limsup of arXiv:2606.05830, '{ Biased Random Walk on $\mathbb Z_+$ with Traps of Linearly Increasing Depth}\footnote{Su'] If $0<\lambda<1$, then \begin{align}
  \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{X_{n,1}}{\log n}=\frac{1}{\log(1/\lambda)},\quad \text{ a.s.,}
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{|X_n|}{\log n}=\frac{1}{\log(1/\lambda)}, \quad
\limsup_{n\to\infty} \frac{|X_n|}{\log n} = \frac{2}{\log(1/\lambda)}, \quad \text{a.s.}.\end{align}

If $\lambda>1$, then
\[
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{X_{n,1}}{\log n}=\frac{1}{\log\lambda},\quad\text{a.s.}.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)
